# p5/contract/foreign -- paths P5 MUST NEVER WRITE.
#
# What this is FOR, precisely. The installer already refuses any destination
# that does not match contract/namespace, so as a guard this file is redundant
# today. It exists for two things that the namespace rule cannot do:
#
#   1. It is the MACHINE-CHECKABLE form of the disjointness claim the whole
#      standalone-product shape rests on. Bar NS-3 in test/run.sh asserts that
#      no pattern in contract/namespace matches any path here. If someone later
#      widens the namespace to reclaim an old-stack path -- say, to ship
#      /usr/sbin/bond-agg again -- that bar goes red and names the collision,
#      instead of the collision being discovered during a rollback at 3am.
#   2. It is defence in depth on the exact defect class this unit was warned
#      about: the deploy runbook seeding /etc/bond/agg_w with an invented
#      20000,15000 (removed in 52a76d3). The installer checks BOTH lists, so
#      re-introducing that write fails at install time with a named violation
#      even if the namespace rule were relaxed.
#
# What this is NOT. It is NOT E7's removal list. E7 must remove the old stack,
# and its list comes from the old package's OWN teardown -- bond-rollback.sh,
# autoratectl off, bondctl revert, engarde stop (p5-execution-handover.md:80).
# That list is U26's to derive and is a SUPERSET of the entries below, which
# were collected only by grepping the frozen baseline for absolute paths:
#   grep -rhoE '/(etc|usr|var|opt|root)/[A-Za-z0-9_./*-]+' p1-autorate/ p2-engarde/ p3-ecod/
# plus the paths deploy/p5's reconciler set writes. Anything the old stack
# creates at RUNTIME rather than naming in its source is absent here.
#
# THE UNIT OF OWNERSHIP FOR A UCI CONFIG FILE IS THE NAMED OBJECT, NOT THE
# FILE. This is forced, not a preference. /etc/config/firewall is GL native and
# is shared by GL, the old stack and P5; if the FILE were the unit, then either
# P5 may never open the bonded UDP port -- the server is then unreachable and
# E3 cannot work -- or P5 claims a file GL owns and "remove precisely P5" stops
# being decidable. The old stack already resolved this the right way: ONE named
# rule object (p2-engarde/bootstrap-bond-server.sh:57-63, `uci set
# firewall.engarde...`), and the handover's removal list names the OBJECT --
# "engarde + bondctl + its firewall rule (`firewall.engarde`)"
# (p5-execution-handover.md:80) -- not the file. So:
#   * the FILE stays on this list. Nothing may ever INSTALL a file there, and
#     the installer refuses one by name.
#   * the OBJECTS P5 owns are declared in contract/paths with kind=uci. Today
#     there is exactly one, `firewall.p5`, and nothing writes it yet.
#   * the objects the OLD stack owns are listed here with a `uci:` pattern, so
#     the disjointness bar checks P5's objects against them too.
# A `uci:` pattern is not an absolute path and therefore never matches a
# filesystem destination; the path matchers skip it by construction.
#
# /etc/config/sqm: P5 DOES NOT REPLACE P1'S WRITES TO IT, IT DROPS THEM.
# P1's shaper controller drives `uci set sqm.$SID.enabled` + `uci commit sqm`
# (p1-autorate/autoratectl:38-39,51-52), where $SID is discovered by matching
# the queue's `.interface` against the tunnel iface (autoratectl:30) -- not a
# fixed object name, which is why there is no `uci:sqm.*` row here: deleting
# the whole sqm config would take GL's native queues with it, and picking out
# the right queue is E7's derivation (U26), not a pattern this file can state.
# What replaces those writes under P5 is named in the record: E4 is "cake +
# cake-autorate on the tunnel iface ... Non-interfering with GL native SQM
# (converge-guard, loud logging)" (p5-execution-handover.md:71), and E7 removes
# the old `sqm.wgclient1` config outright (:80, and e4-shaping-in-dag.md:22).
# So P5 shapes its own tunnel and OBSERVES GL native SQM rather than driving
# it. If E4 turns out to need persisted shaper settings, the E0-level answer is
# already fixed: they go inside the P5 namespace (/etc/p5/* or /etc/config/p5),
# never into `sqm`. Owner of that residual question: U22 (E4).
#
# FORMAT: origin|pattern
#   origin  p1 | p2 | p3 | p4 | gl   (which stack the path belongs to)
#   pattern POSIX `case` glob, absolute production path, or `uci:<object>` for
#           a named UCI object (see above)

# ---- P1: cake + cake-autorate ----------------------------------------------
p1|/etc/init.d/cake-autorate
p1|/etc/init.d/sqm
p1|/etc/hotplug.d/iface/98-wg-autorate
p1|/etc/hotplug.d/iface/99-tether-autorate
p1|/root/cake-autorate
p1|/root/cake-autorate/*
p1|/root/autoratectl
p1|/root/bootstrap-autorate.sh
p1|/var/log/cake-autorate.wg.log
p1|/etc/tether-reflectors
p1|/etc/wg-reflectors
p1|/etc/wg-logical

# ---- P2: engarde + bondctl v2.8 --------------------------------------------
p2|/usr/sbin/engarde-client
p2|/usr/sbin/engarde-server
p2|/etc/init.d/engarde-client
p2|/etc/init.d/engarde-server
p2|/root/engarde-client-arm64
p2|/root/engarde-server-arm64
p2|/root/engarde-server-armv7
p2|/root/bondctl

# ---- P3 / P4 / the deploy/p5 reconciler set: the bond namespace ------------
# One namespace, three generations of owner, all of it foreign to P5.
p3|/etc/bond
p3|/etc/bond/*
p3|/var/run/bond
p3|/var/run/bond/*
p3|/usr/sbin/bondctl
p3|/usr/sbin/bond-ecod
p3|/usr/sbin/bond-xctl
p3|/usr/sbin/bond-watchdog
p4|/usr/sbin/bond-agg
p3|/etc/init.d/bond-agg
p3|/etc/init.d/bond-ecod
p3|/etc/init.d/bond-watchdog
p3|/etc/hotplug.d/iface/97-bond

# ---- the old stack's procd rc.d flags --------------------------------------
# Derived from the /etc/init.d rows above, not collected separately: enabling
# any of those services writes /etc/rc.d/S<pri><svc> and K<pri><svc>. The grep
# in the header could not see them -- they are created at ENABLE time, never
# named in the sources -- which is exactly the class this file said it was
# missing. Listed so the installer refuses them and so the disjointness bar has
# both sides of the /etc/rc.d namespace to compare.
p1|/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]cake-autorate
p1|/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]sqm
p2|/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]engarde-*
p3|/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]bond-*

# ---- named UCI objects the old stack owns ----------------------------------
p2|uci:firewall.engarde

# ---- GL.iNet native: non-interference (handover section 1) -----------------
# P5 must be non-interfering with GL native features. These are the config
# surfaces the old stack touched or the product could be tempted to touch.
gl|/etc/config/network
gl|/etc/config/firewall
gl|/etc/config/wireless
gl|/etc/config/sqm
gl|/etc/config/dhcp
gl|/etc/config/glconfig
gl|/etc/init.d/firewall
gl|/etc/init.d/network

# ---- THE MANAGEMENT PATH: the branch the operator is sitting on ------------
# The server (GL-MT2500 at ${SERVER_PC_IP}) has NO PHYSICAL ACCESS -- no console,
# no recovery button, no power-cycle into failsafe (ROADMAP, STANDING
# CONSTRAINT). Every deploy step reaches it over SSH. So the set of files that
# carry that SSH session is a distinct class from "GL native config we should
# be polite about": writing one of these is not a non-interference violation,
# it is the loss of the box.
#
# The class is: the SSH daemon and its config, the network stack that routes
# the session, the firewall that admits it, and the boot flags that bring any
# of them up. The first four rows below are already above under `gl` and are
# NOT repeated -- a duplicate pattern would make the disjointness bar's output
# ambiguous about which row fired. What is added here is what those rows did
# not cover.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL CONSEQUENCE, and it is the whole reason this section is worth
# writing: p5_check_dest consults this file BEFORE contract/namespace, so no
# filemap row, no packaging mistake and no widened namespace can put a P5 file
# on any of these paths. E0's installer is therefore structurally incapable of
# touching the management path. That is a mechanism, not a promise, and bar
# MG-1 demonstrates it by packaging a file at each of these destinations and
# asserting exit 4 with the refusal naming the foreign origin.
#
# It does NOT cover the case that actually needs a deadman: a step that writes
# nothing here but still cuts the path -- `uci commit firewall` after touching
# firewall.p5, `/etc/init.d/network restart`, stopping the service that carries
# the tunnel the session rides. Those are E5's and E7's, they are actions
# rather than destinations, and no path list can catch them. That is what
# /usr/sbin/p5-deadman is for, and CONTRACT.md states the rule that binds them.
gl|/etc/config/dropbear
gl|/etc/init.d/dropbear
gl|/etc/dropbear
gl|/etc/dropbear/*
gl|/etc/ssh
gl|/etc/ssh/*
gl|/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]dropbear
gl|/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]network
gl|/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]firewall
gl|/etc/config/system
